# highdock-rs (crate: `highdock_rs`)

One package for a HiDock recorder at `projects/highdock-rs/`.

It opens the recorder, downloads a recording, decodes the file, and writes speaker lines.
`Transcript` lists each speaker, then the conversation in time order.
`write_json` writes that document. It does not store the recording.

## Boundary

Do not add storage, a knowledge graph, or an application UI here.
The speaker roster is process memory. The crate does not write it, and transcript JSON has no voice vector.

Do not add card format, factory reset, firmware upload, or a USB port reset.
A port reset during a transfer can wedge the recorder firmware.

## Protocol

The wire format is Jensen on bulk OUT `0x01` and bulk IN `0x82`.

One command is active at a time. A protocol fault poisons the session.
The caller drops `Device` and reconnects the recorder. Do not clear the poison
flag to continue the same USB claim.

## USB

`detach_and_claim_interface(0)` releases a kernel driver on Linux for interface 0
of the matched recorder only.

IN reads stay at 8192 bytes. A larger read can sit forever on an exact H1E frame.

## Audio

A P1 file is Ogg Opus at 16 kHz or MPEG-1 Layer III at 48 kHz.
Decode either one to 16 kHz mono `f32` before Polyvoice.

Reject an empty file, a file over 256 MiB, and audio longer than one hour.
A final Ogg page that ends early keeps the packets before that page.
Do not log sample values.

Diarization weights come from the Polyvoice registry. The call verifies them.

The Parakeet directory is a local path the caller passes. Do not download it
inside this crate. A hostile ONNX file in that directory is trusted input.

## Tests

`cargo test` uses a fake transport and decodes a generated Opus page.
It does not open a recorder and it does not load a speech model.
